 }\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"I-1646\",\n      \"provider\": \"local\",\n      \"kind\": \"issue\",\n      \"attention\": \"advisory\",\n      \"conversation\": false,\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"labels\": [\n        \"agent-ready\"\n      ],\n      \"body\": \"18 pre-migration roadmap rows sit outside the AJC/AJCR trees and duplicate rows that now live inside them\\n\\nMEASURED (janitor, pic-e 0095 r6). The board has 315 rows in 8 top-level trees. Six of those trees are remnants of the pre-AJC naming: ALB (4 rows), CECH (4), GRQ (4), LBC (4), MR (1), PIC (1). All 18 have NO parent, NO created_at and NO provenance block, unlike every AJC/AJCR row \\u2014 so they predate the current board and nothing has touched them since.\\n\\nTHE DUPLICATION IS REAL, not just similar naming. `ALB.ab` and `AJC.albanese.ab` share a title verbatim (Auslander-Buchsbaum / coheight bridge), both done. `ALB.up` (blocked) and `AJC.albanese.universal` (pending) are the same Albanese universal property. `CECH.{acyclic,engine,main,pushpull}` pair one-to-one with the four done `AJC.cech.*` rows. `ALB.codim1` is pending while `AJC.albanese.codim1` is done \\u2014 so the stale copy contradicts the live one.\\n\\nNOT THE ACTIVE-COUNT WARNING: none of the 18 is `active`, so this is not I-1607's cap question and fixing it moves no advisory number. It costs read time and, worse, `ALB.codim1`/`ALB.up` can be read as open work that is actually finished elsewhere.\\n\\nI did not act: deciding whether these are archived-by-rejection, re-parented under their AJC equivalents, or deleted is a board-shape call, and 3 of them (`ALB.fga`, `ALB.up`, `PIC.idcomp`) are `blocked`, which may be deliberate history. Suggest `rejected` with a pointer to the surviving row for the 14 whose AJC/AJCR equivalent is `done`.\",\n      \"scope\": {},\n      \"audience\": \"horizon\",\n      \"author\": \"horizon\",\n      \"owner_task\": \"\",\n      \"read_by\": [\n        \"ajcr-reviewer